 "beats": [
  {
   "type": "title",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "globe",
   "text": "Why do so many ships avoid the Red Sea today?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Because of attacks at its southern gate, a narrow strait called Bab-el-Mandeb: the Gate of Grief."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Where is that?",
   "scene": "today",
   "date": "Today",
   "place": "Bab-el-Mandeb"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Between Yemen and the Horn of Africa, with Djibouti and Eritrea on the other side."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "At its narrowest it's about twenty-six kilometres wide, and the island of Perim splits it in two.",
   "scene": "strait",
   "date": "",
   "place": "Bab-el-Mandeb"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Why the Gate of Grief?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Some say for its dangerous waters. Others tell an old legend about an earthquake that drowned many people."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Was it always important?",
   "scene": "mochamap",
   "date": "1600s",
   "place": "Mocha"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "For centuries. Coffee from Yemen and Ethiopia left from the port of Mocha, just inside the strait. That's where the name mocha comes from."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In its heyday, Dutch and English traders came for its coffee.",
   "scene": "mocha",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.5,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And who controlled it?",
   "scene": "aden",
   "date": "1839",
   "place": "Aden",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.4,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.5,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In 1839 Britain took Aden, nearby. After the Suez Canal opened, this became the sea road to India, and Britain stayed until 1967."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "So what's happening now?",
   "scene": "navy",
   "date": "Since late 2023",
   "place": "Red Sea",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.5,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Since late 2023, Yemen's Houthi rebels have attacked ships passing through. Warships came to escort them."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Many shipping lines decided to sail around Africa instead. That adds one to two weeks to a trip from Asia to Europe.",
   "scene": "reroute",
   "date": "",
   "place": "Cape of Good Hope"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Has it calmed down?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Not for long. After a pause, the threats returned in 2026. Traffic through Suez is still far below what it was."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Next time: the Panama Canal. Like the video and follow History Sketches."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And tell us in the comments what you think!"
  },
  {
   "type": "end",
   "text": "Next: the Panama Canal",
   "sub": "Follow History Sketches"
  }
 ]
}